  "preamble": "Assibilation of [d] to [dz]: the stop develops a fricative release, usually before high vowels. In Late Middle Japanese /du/ was pronounced [dzu] (Old Japanese midu 'water' > midzu, later mizu), in Quebec French /d/ is regularly affricated to [dz] before /i y j ɥ/ (difficile [dzifisɪl]), and in Swati and Phuthi Proto-Nguni *d became dz (Proto-Nguni *-de 'long' > Swati -dze, while Zulu and Xhosa keep -de). In the Romanian second palatalization Latin d before i became dz: dīcō 'I say' > Aromanian dzãc, Moldavian, Banat and Maramureș Romanian [d͡zɨk] (standard zic after the later dz > z), vēndis 'you sell' > Old Romanian vindzi.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Assibilation",
